    "knowledge_point": "Applying the Limit Definition of the Exponential Function",
    "problem": "${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{\\pi x}{n})}^{2n}=$",
    "worked_solution": "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{u}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{u}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nHence, with $u=\\pi x$, we get\n\n$$\n{e}^{\\pi x}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{\\pi x}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{\\pi x}{n})}^{2n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{\\pi x}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{\\pi x}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={({e}^{\\pi x})}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\pi x}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
